  "worked_solution": "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${q}_{1}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number ${q}_{3}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{3})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{q}_{1}^{2}-1}{8} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {q}_{1}^{2}-1 & =2 \\\\ {q}_{1}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {q}_{1} & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we take the root ${q}_{1}=\\sqrt{3}$ because $1\\le {q}_{1}\\le 3.$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{q}_{3}^{2}-1}{8} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {q}_{3}^{2}-1 & =6 \\\\ {q}_{3}^{2} & =7 \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we take the root ${q}_{3}=\\sqrt{7}$ because $1\\le {q}_{3}\\le 3.$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}IQR & ={q}_{3}-{q}_{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & ≈0.914\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.",
